WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.574 --> 00:00:02.621
- I definitely grew up being like,

00:00:02.621 --> 00:00:03.772
I've got to get good grades,

00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:05.071
I've got to get a good job,

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.282
I've got to get married
at a certain point.

00:00:07.282 --> 00:00:10.342
I know if I'm not given sort of obstacles,

00:00:10.342 --> 00:00:12.734
I don't really, I get kind of lazy.

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:15.336
I don't need to base my sense of self on

00:00:15.336 --> 00:00:17.296
what stranger's opinions of me are.

00:00:17.296 --> 00:00:19.923
Once your technique
gets to a certain level,

00:00:19.923 --> 00:00:22.129
the only thing that
separates you from others

00:00:22.129 --> 00:00:24.511
is your quirks, or even flaws.

00:00:24.511 --> 00:00:28.512
I think it's a really
good tool too, just to

00:00:28.512 --> 00:00:31.077
learn something from
everyone you work with.

00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:33.624
- She's an actress, film
director and producer,

00:00:33.624 --> 00:00:36.848
and carries duel American
and Israeli citizenship.

00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:39.276
Her performance in Black
Swan received widespread

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:42.646
critical acclaim, and won
her her first Academy Award

00:00:42.646 --> 00:00:44.027
for Best Actress.

00:00:44.027 --> 00:00:46.120
She was cast as Padmé Amidala

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.343
in the Star Wars prequel trilogy.

00:00:48.343 --> 00:00:50.196
She's Natalie Portman, and here's my take

00:00:50.196 --> 00:00:52.034
on her top ten rules for success.

00:00:52.034 --> 00:00:53.705
My personal favorite is number eight,

00:00:53.705 --> 00:00:55.447
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:55.447 --> 00:00:57.269
for some bonus footage.

00:00:57.269 --> 00:00:59.352
(whoosh)

00:01:00.265 --> 00:01:03.265
- It's very hard to separate the ...

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.052
You know the things that
other people want for you,

00:01:09.052 --> 00:01:10.864
the values that society tells you

00:01:10.864 --> 00:01:13.013
you're supposed to go after,

00:01:13.013 --> 00:01:16.586
and the things that are
really meaningful for you.

00:01:16.586 --> 00:01:18.585
I definitely grew up being like,

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:19.680
I've got to get good grades,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:20.928
I've got to get a good job,

00:01:20.928 --> 00:01:24.242
I've got to get married
at a certain point.

00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:26.097
You feel like there's these things that

00:01:26.097 --> 00:01:29.930
you're supposed to do
and that you have to do.

00:01:31.336 --> 00:01:34.602
It's an amazing shift;
I'm sure many of you

00:01:34.602 --> 00:01:36.102
have gotten there,

00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:39.981
or will get there if you haven't but,

00:01:39.981 --> 00:01:43.706
it's an amazing thing when
one day you're just like,

00:01:43.706 --> 00:01:46.762
oh this is what is important for me,

00:01:46.762 --> 00:01:50.429
and I don't care what
they say, I don't care

00:01:53.554 --> 00:01:55.688
why other people think I'm
doing it or not doing it,

00:01:55.688 --> 00:01:57.688
the rest is sort of ego.

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:04.863
- Can you name that
moment when that happened?

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.690
- I don't know I think it
really started happening

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.690
when I was doing parts that required

00:02:15.195 --> 00:02:17.778
a real sort of emersion for me.

00:02:19.351 --> 00:02:23.308
Working on things like Goya's Ghost or

00:02:23.308 --> 00:02:26.695
V for Vendetta, The Other Boleyn Girl,

00:02:26.695 --> 00:02:29.195
I did so much sort of like ...

00:02:30.948 --> 00:02:34.460
I got so into the
characters and the stories

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.983
and all the background research

00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:40.316
and really living in it, and it made the

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:43.182
outcome

00:02:44.962 --> 00:02:47.776
not important, when it did
well, when it didn't do well,

00:02:47.776 --> 00:02:50.385
when it got positive
feedback, when it didn't

00:02:50.385 --> 00:02:54.218
it was like, all that stuff
faded away 'cause I had this

00:02:54.218 --> 00:02:56.749
experience and the experience was like,

00:02:56.749 --> 00:03:00.122
oh you can't take away from
me the fact that I spent

00:03:00.122 --> 00:03:03.482
two months going to the Prado
every day and living with

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:07.277
the Goya and El Greco
and Velazquez paintings.

00:03:07.277 --> 00:03:11.834
That was an experience
forever that I will never

00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:12.751
forget and,

00:03:15.816 --> 00:03:19.754
the fact that four people
saw the movie of course.

00:03:19.754 --> 00:03:21.813
You know, obviously when
you care about something

00:03:21.813 --> 00:03:24.362
you want people to engage
with it and it's important

00:03:24.362 --> 00:03:27.495
but it wasn't devastating because

00:03:27.495 --> 00:03:30.912
I had something that was with me forever.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.694
So it changed the way I chose projects

00:03:34.694 --> 00:03:36.423
'cause it wasn't about,

00:03:36.423 --> 00:03:38.263
you know it's the worst
when people are like,

00:03:38.263 --> 00:03:40.372
oh, this is going to be really popular

00:03:40.372 --> 00:03:42.891
or this is going to be an
Oscar movie, and you're like,

00:03:42.891 --> 00:03:45.588
bad sign, bad sign, bad sign.

00:03:45.588 --> 00:03:48.983
Why is this meaningful for
me at this point in my life

00:03:48.983 --> 00:03:52.097
that I want to live this experience?

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:54.979
People told me that Black
Swan was an artistic risk,

00:03:54.979 --> 00:03:58.181
a scary challenge to try
to portray a professional

00:03:58.181 --> 00:04:00.733
ballet dancer, but it
didn't feel like courage

00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:02.541
or daring that drew me into it.

00:04:02.541 --> 00:04:05.587
I was so oblivious to my
own limits that I did things

00:04:05.587 --> 00:04:08.183
I was woefully unprepared to do.

00:04:08.183 --> 00:04:10.603
So the very inexperienced that in college

00:04:10.603 --> 00:04:12.306
that had made me feel insecure,

00:04:12.306 --> 00:04:14.428
and made me want to play by other's rules,

00:04:14.428 --> 00:04:17.155
now was making me actually take risks

00:04:17.155 --> 00:04:19.512
I didn't even realize were risks.

00:04:19.512 --> 00:04:21.385
When Darren asked me if I could do ballet

00:04:21.385 --> 00:04:23.792
I told him that I was
basically a ballerina.

00:04:23.792 --> 00:04:26.797
Which by the way, I
wholeheartedly believed.

00:04:26.797 --> 00:04:30.356
When it quickly became clear
in preparing for the film

00:04:30.356 --> 00:04:33.545
that I was maybe 15 years
away from being a ballerina,

00:04:33.545 --> 00:04:36.316
it made me work a million
times harder, and of course,

00:04:36.316 --> 00:04:38.317
the magic of cinema
and body doubles helped

00:04:38.317 --> 00:04:39.922
the final effect.

00:04:39.922 --> 00:04:43.081
But the point is, if I had
known my own limitations,

00:04:43.081 --> 00:04:45.005
I never would have taken the risk,

00:04:45.005 --> 00:04:47.138
and the risk led to one
of my greatest artistic

00:04:47.138 --> 00:04:49.812
and personal experiences,
and that I not only felt

00:04:49.812 --> 00:04:53.103
completely free, I also met
my husband during filming.

00:04:53.103 --> 00:04:55.210
- [Interviewer] And what
compels you to get involved

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.968
in a project; I know that's
kind of a hard question

00:04:56.968 --> 00:04:59.115
because it's probably so
many different things.

00:04:59.115 --> 00:05:02.486
When you think about projects that you

00:05:02.486 --> 00:05:04.239
get involved with, what are some of the

00:05:04.239 --> 00:05:05.863
key elements?

00:05:05.863 --> 00:05:10.306
- Well I think it can be
really anything that gets

00:05:10.306 --> 00:05:11.556
you interested.

00:05:12.487 --> 00:05:14.906
I just want to make sure
I'm going to be challenged

00:05:14.906 --> 00:05:18.238
the whole time because,
I know if I'm not given

00:05:18.238 --> 00:05:20.758
sort of obstacles, I don't really,

00:05:20.758 --> 00:05:24.673
I get kind of lazy, I don't
really engage in the way

00:05:24.673 --> 00:05:26.130
I want to, so.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.019
If I know something's
going to constantly be

00:05:28.019 --> 00:05:30.978
throwing challenges at me, not in like,

00:05:30.978 --> 00:05:32.492
I want to have fun, you know.

00:05:32.492 --> 00:05:35.293
I always want to enjoy what I'm doing.

00:05:35.293 --> 00:05:38.222
Something that is going to be,

00:05:38.222 --> 00:05:39.738
but that's part of the fun I think,

00:05:39.738 --> 00:05:43.290
is getting all of the challenges of it.

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.331
- [Man] Do you read criticism of yourself?

00:05:45.331 --> 00:05:48.857
- No, I used to, I used
to, and then I realized

00:05:48.857 --> 00:05:50.220
it wasn't helpful.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.183
- [Man] Positive or negative?

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:52.285
- Mm-hmm.

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:53.311
- [Man] It's just a distraction?

00:05:53.311 --> 00:05:54.879
- Yeah.

00:05:54.879 --> 00:05:56.926
I don't look at anything
anymore that's written,

00:05:56.926 --> 00:06:00.367
whether it's interviews or I mean ...

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:03.867
It's also just too much focus on yourself.

00:06:05.534 --> 00:06:07.549
I am far from the center of the world

00:06:07.549 --> 00:06:09.511
and I don't need to like,

00:06:09.511 --> 00:06:11.247
I need to read about other things.

00:06:11.247 --> 00:06:13.944
There's a lot of really good
things to read about and,

00:06:13.944 --> 00:06:15.780
I know about myself and

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:17.214
if someone makes a mistake about me

00:06:17.214 --> 00:06:19.229
I'm just going to get upset, so.

00:06:19.229 --> 00:06:20.929
Or if they say something negative

00:06:20.929 --> 00:06:23.703
I'm going to get upset and
if they say something nice,

00:06:23.703 --> 00:06:26.294
that's friendly but I
also don't need to care,

00:06:26.294 --> 00:06:29.229
I don't need to base my sense of self on

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:31.226
what strangers opinions of me are.

00:06:31.226 --> 00:06:34.616
It's important to me that
my friends and my family

00:06:34.616 --> 00:06:37.783
think that I'm behaving correctly and,

00:06:39.643 --> 00:06:42.400
they'll let me know if I'm out of line and

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:45.564
everyone else's opinions like,

00:06:45.564 --> 00:06:47.255
it's not my business.

00:06:47.255 --> 00:06:49.656
By the time I got to making Black Swan,

00:06:49.656 --> 00:06:52.240
the experience was entirely my own.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.915
I felt immune to the worst
things anyone could say

00:06:54.915 --> 00:06:57.590
or write about me, and to
whether an audience felt

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:00.255
like going to see my movie or not.

00:07:00.255 --> 00:07:03.323
It was instructive for me
to see that ballet dancers,

00:07:03.323 --> 00:07:05.564
for ballet dancers once
your technique gets to

00:07:05.564 --> 00:07:08.594
a certain level, the only
thing that separates you

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:12.149
from others is your quirks, or even flaws.

00:07:12.149 --> 00:07:14.172
One ballerina was famous
for how she turned

00:07:14.172 --> 00:07:15.753
slightly off-balance.

00:07:15.753 --> 00:07:18.050
You can never be the best technically.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.488
Someone will always have a higher jump,

00:07:20.488 --> 00:07:22.299
or a more beautiful line.

00:07:22.299 --> 00:07:24.190
The only think you can be the best at

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:26.738
is developing your own self.

00:07:26.738 --> 00:07:27.905
Writing, I ...

00:07:31.071 --> 00:07:35.130
I think everyone probably
has a different process but,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.630
my sort of experience is that,

00:07:39.896 --> 00:07:42.021
I have an idea for a story in my head

00:07:42.021 --> 00:07:44.587
for a very, very long
time and little things

00:07:44.587 --> 00:07:45.804
glob onto it.

00:07:45.804 --> 00:07:48.677
I don't write any notes or anything down,

00:07:48.677 --> 00:07:51.284
and then after a couple years,

00:07:51.284 --> 00:07:54.950
it sort of just comes out in one spurt.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.730
It just sort of molds
itself in my head and

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.679
then just comes out.

00:07:59.679 --> 00:08:03.693
And so that is what
happened with both occasions

00:08:03.693 --> 00:08:06.699
and then once I get my first draft out,

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:10.190
I send it out to ten people I trust,

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:11.863
which I know a lot of writers don't do,

00:08:11.863 --> 00:08:13.868
this is not a normal process.

00:08:13.868 --> 00:08:15.903
A lot of people really
tinker with it a lot

00:08:15.903 --> 00:08:17.570
but I do my like ...

00:08:18.783 --> 00:08:22.950
This crazy first draft and
then get people's feedback.

00:08:24.982 --> 00:08:28.946
Then start doing all this
very, very detailed work

00:08:28.946 --> 00:08:32.982
using the feedback and then
it sort of inspires me to;

00:08:32.982 --> 00:08:34.996
because it gives me a kind of perspective

00:08:34.996 --> 00:08:37.106
that I don't have because,

00:08:37.106 --> 00:08:40.939
I think this story is
in my brain so long that

00:08:42.001 --> 00:08:43.601
you get too close to it.

00:08:43.601 --> 00:08:46.518
Amazing thing about Darren is that,

00:08:48.662 --> 00:08:50.745
there's not the ego that;

00:08:53.233 --> 00:08:55.579
there's some people
who just, they're like,

00:08:55.579 --> 00:08:59.484
"I'm the boss, I'm going
to make the rules."

00:08:59.484 --> 00:09:03.000
He asked questions, he listened and took

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.421
ideas when they were good
and when they weren't good

00:09:04.421 --> 00:09:06.372
he's like, that's not a
good idea and you just

00:09:06.372 --> 00:09:08.174
wouldn't do it and that's fine

00:09:08.174 --> 00:09:10.381
and that's always what
you're doing as a cast

00:09:10.381 --> 00:09:12.193
or crew member is like,

00:09:12.193 --> 00:09:14.939
you bring your ideas and if

00:09:14.939 --> 00:09:17.341
they're right, great,
and if they're not, not.

00:09:17.341 --> 00:09:19.563
I've definitely worked
with people who are like,

00:09:19.563 --> 00:09:21.095
don't want to hear anything.

00:09:21.095 --> 00:09:25.782
That's not your job, just go
do what I tell you to do, which

00:09:25.782 --> 00:09:28.153
makes it less interesting
of course and then of course

00:09:28.153 --> 00:09:30.072
there's people who listen too much and

00:09:30.072 --> 00:09:32.111
they water down their own
vision which is, of course

00:09:32.111 --> 00:09:34.444
is not a problem for Darren.

00:09:35.366 --> 00:09:38.665
That's the joy; also, that
was something I learned

00:09:38.665 --> 00:09:42.494
when I was directing too,
to just have an open ear

00:09:42.494 --> 00:09:44.498
and use people's best suggestions.

00:09:44.498 --> 00:09:47.679
You have 100 people who are there for you,

00:09:47.679 --> 00:09:50.313
to help you create your
vision and sometimes they have

00:09:50.313 --> 00:09:51.402
a good idea.

00:09:51.402 --> 00:09:52.737
- [Reporter] What do you
want from a director?

00:09:52.737 --> 00:09:55.070
- I really, I love feedback.

00:09:59.590 --> 00:10:02.442
I think artistic honesty is,

00:10:02.442 --> 00:10:05.874
the key and I mean Darren is exactly that.

00:10:05.874 --> 00:10:07.141
He'll tell you when it's not going well,

00:10:07.141 --> 00:10:08.263
and he'll tell you when it's going well

00:10:08.263 --> 00:10:09.607
and you can trust that it's going well

00:10:09.607 --> 00:10:12.079
when he says it is because
he'll tell you if it's not.

00:10:12.079 --> 00:10:16.246
He always had a million
different ideas for every scene.

00:10:17.508 --> 00:10:20.066
Was always on top of
it and then at the end

00:10:20.066 --> 00:10:23.199
would always say, do
this one for yourself.

00:10:23.199 --> 00:10:26.209
The last one, do it for
yourself which was just

00:10:26.209 --> 00:10:28.899
exactly the key to the character because

00:10:28.899 --> 00:10:31.319
it was what Vincent says to,

00:10:31.319 --> 00:10:33.392
Vincent's character says
to my character which is,

00:10:33.392 --> 00:10:34.909
find your own pleasure.

00:10:34.909 --> 00:10:38.609
Which is at the root of
being an artist, which

00:10:38.609 --> 00:10:39.760
Darren hands it to me.
- Find your

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:40.698
own pleasure?

00:10:40.698 --> 00:10:42.925
- Find your own pleasure,
because you're trying to;

00:10:42.925 --> 00:10:45.520
so often as an actor you're
pleasing your director.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.638
You know the kids at
the pageants at the end

00:10:47.638 --> 00:10:49.115
when they look at their mom right after

00:10:49.115 --> 00:10:50.599
they finish doing their song?

00:10:50.599 --> 00:10:52.095
Sometimes you're like that as an actor.

00:10:52.095 --> 00:10:56.668
You finish the scene,
you're like, how was it?

00:10:56.668 --> 00:10:59.407
When they're saying, just
do it for yourself it's,

00:10:59.407 --> 00:11:02.351
a whole new world opens
and Darren gave that to me,

00:11:02.351 --> 00:11:05.640
because directors often say
do a freebie or a free one.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.996
But just putting those words,
articulating it in that

00:11:07.996 --> 00:11:10.712
particular way, do it for yourself.

00:11:10.712 --> 00:11:12.629
I think it's really ...

00:11:14.995 --> 00:11:18.528
I think it's a really good tool too, to

00:11:18.528 --> 00:11:21.640
learn something from
everyone you work with.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:25.307
Every person you work
with, steal from them.

00:11:28.078 --> 00:11:29.845
I think stealing from
people when you're acting

00:11:29.845 --> 00:11:31.762
is really easy, because

00:11:33.227 --> 00:11:36.727
it's always going to be
funneled through you.

00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:40.026
It's not plagiarism in any,

00:11:40.026 --> 00:11:44.500
no one will ever know, unless
you're really not good.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:48.030
At the risk of sounding like
a Miss America contestant,

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.330
the most fulfilling things I've
experienced have truly been

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:54.128
the human interactions,
spending time with women

00:11:54.128 --> 00:11:56.133
in village banks in Mexico with FICA,

00:11:56.133 --> 00:11:58.817
microfinance organization,
meeting young women

00:11:58.817 --> 00:12:00.756
who were the first and
only in their communities

00:12:00.756 --> 00:12:03.145
to attend secondary school in rural Kenya

00:12:03.145 --> 00:12:04.581
with Free the Children,
a group that builds

00:12:04.581 --> 00:12:07.646
sustainable schools in
developing countries,

00:12:07.646 --> 00:12:10.601
trekking with gorilla
conservationists in Rwanda.

00:12:10.601 --> 00:12:13.535
It's a cliche, because it's
true, that helping others

00:12:13.535 --> 00:12:15.730
ends up helping you more than anyone.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:17.719
Getting out of your own concerns

00:12:17.719 --> 00:12:20.321
and caring about someone
else's life for awhile

00:12:20.321 --> 00:12:23.764
reminds you that you are not
the center of the universe.

00:12:23.764 --> 00:12:26.904
That in the ways we are generous or not.

00:12:26.904 --> 00:12:29.317
We can change the course
of someone's life.

00:12:29.317 --> 00:12:31.610
Even at work, the small feats of kindness

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.834
crew members, directors,
fellow actors have shown me,

00:12:34.834 --> 00:12:37.584
have had the most lasting impact.

00:12:39.681 --> 00:12:41.035
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:41.035 --> 00:12:43.711
I made this video because
you guys asked me to.

00:12:43.711 --> 00:12:45.667
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur you want me

00:12:45.667 --> 00:12:48.072
to profile next, leave
in the comments below

00:12:48.072 --> 00:12:49.226
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:49.226 --> 00:12:50.887
I'd also love to know
which of Natalie Portman's

00:12:50.887 --> 00:12:53.464
top ten rules had the
biggest impact on you.

00:12:53.464 --> 00:12:55.039
How did they move you, how did they change

00:12:55.039 --> 00:12:56.255
your mindset?

00:12:56.255 --> 00:12:58.442
Leave it in the comments
below and I will join

00:12:58.442 --> 00:12:59.358
in the discussion.

00:12:59.358 --> 00:13:00.811
Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:00.811 --> 00:13:03.856
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

00:13:03.856 --> 00:13:05.939
(whoosh)

00:13:09.608 --> 00:13:11.562
- I think it's always
really helpful to just

00:13:11.562 --> 00:13:15.312
incorporate anything
that's happening in life

00:13:16.839 --> 00:13:18.011
into your work.

00:13:18.011 --> 00:13:21.011
Obviously in a way that makes sense.

00:13:21.876 --> 00:13:24.621
I think acknowledging it and sort of

00:13:24.621 --> 00:13:27.417
threading it into what you're
doing is always helpful.

00:13:27.417 --> 00:13:30.667
As we get older, we get more realistic.

00:13:32.542 --> 00:13:36.319
That includes about our own
abilities or lack thereof

00:13:36.319 --> 00:13:38.984
and that realism does us no favors.

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:40.887
People always talk
about diving into things

00:13:40.887 --> 00:13:43.358
you're afraid of; that
never worked for me.

00:13:43.358 --> 00:13:46.553
If I'm afraid, I run away,
and I would probably urge

00:13:46.553 --> 00:13:48.548
my child to do the same.

00:13:48.548 --> 00:13:50.970
Fear protects us in many ways.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.234
What has served me is diving
into my own obliviousness.

00:13:54.234 --> 00:13:56.194
Being more confident than I should be,

00:13:56.194 --> 00:13:58.915
which everyone should tends
to decry in American kids,

00:13:58.915 --> 00:14:00.856
and those of us who
have been grade-inflated

00:14:00.856 --> 00:14:03.358
and ego-inflated, well
it can be a good thing

00:14:03.358 --> 00:14:06.249
if it makes you try things
you never might have tried.

00:14:06.249 --> 00:14:08.459
Your inexperience is an
asset and will allow you

00:14:08.459 --> 00:14:10.997
to think in original, unconventional ways.

00:14:10.997 --> 00:14:14.480
Accept your lack of knowledge,
and use it as your asset.

00:14:14.480 --> 00:14:16.587
I know a famous violinist who told me

00:14:16.587 --> 00:14:18.509
that he can't compose, because he knows

00:14:18.509 --> 00:14:20.451
too many pieces so when he starts thinking

00:14:20.451 --> 00:14:24.638
of a note, an existing piece
immediately comes to mind.

00:14:24.638 --> 00:14:26.808
Just starting out, one
of your biggest strengths

00:14:26.808 --> 00:14:29.111
is not knowing how things
are supposed to be.

00:14:29.111 --> 00:14:32.058
You can compose freely because
your mind isn't cluttered

00:14:32.058 --> 00:14:34.751
with too many pieces, and
you don't take for granted

00:14:34.751 --> 00:14:36.313
the way things are.

00:14:36.313 --> 00:14:38.157
The only way you know how to do things,

00:14:38.157 --> 00:14:39.504
is your own way.

00:14:39.504 --> 00:14:40.799
- [Male] What's your ambition now?

00:14:40.799 --> 00:14:42.525
- I think to ...

00:14:42.525 --> 00:14:45.525
To get back to enjoying not working.

00:14:48.157 --> 00:14:50.703
I've been working so much
and I think I've gotten

00:14:50.703 --> 00:14:51.953
into a sort of,

00:14:54.128 --> 00:14:58.211
workaholism that is
unhealthy so, I'm learning to

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:03.415
enjoy my life without having
to be busy all the time.

